<p>On 4 October 2026 I closed the second volume of Sunday metaphysics. The first sketched a map of questions and traditions. This one stays with their common knot: what consciousness is, what reality is, and on what grounds we can say anything about either. It is not a choice of a winning teaching, and it is not a collection of encouraging sayings. The motto is depth without a shortcut, openness without credulity. The full text is in the <a href="library.php?slug=metafyzika-2">library</a>.</p>
<p>The main question is simple and easy to spoil. When my consciousness changes, does reality change, or only the way reality appears to me? Until both possibilities are drawn precisely, the answer is only a slogan.</p>
<h2>Three levels that must not be fused</h2>
<p>Ontology asks what exists and what character it has. Epistemology asks how we know it and what entitles our belief. Ethics asks how we ought to act. From the fact that something exists it does not follow, without further premises, that it is good or that we ought to be guided by it.</p>
<p>Metaphysical claims are not all directly testable by a single experiment. That does not make them equally good. We can ask whether they contradict themselves, how they explain what we already know, and whether they create more problems than they solve. Caution should lead to a better argument, not to the idea that reasons do not matter. The same standard applies to physicalism and to spiritual systems.</p>
<h2>Consciousness is not one word</h2>
<p>The word consciousness covers several different phenomena. Wakefulness is a state of the organism, not a full description of experience. Subjective experience means that a being lives through a state from the inside: pain is not only the processing of a signal. Dreaming shows that experience cannot simply be identified with wakefulness. Access consciousness is the availability of information for thought and report. Self-awareness is the ability to relate experience to oneself. Attention selects contents; consciousness includes their lived form. Qualia are the subjective qualities of experience, such as how red looks or how coffee tastes. The word itself does not decide that experience is non-physical.</p>
<p>Before any claim about consciousness it is worth asking: are we talking about wakefulness, about experience, about the availability of information, or about awareness of oneself?</p>
<p>David Chalmers, in <a href="https://consc.net/papers/facing.pdf"><em>Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness</em></a> (<em>Journal of Consciousness Studies</em>, 1995), separated the so-called easy problems from the hard one. The easy problems are not practically easy. They can, however, be stated as questions of mechanisms and functions: how the brain discriminates stimuli, integrates information and guides behaviour. The hard problem asks why and how these processes are accompanied by experience. Joseph Levine, in 1983 in the <em>Pacific Philosophical Quarterly</em>, named the explanatory gap: even if we knew the physical mechanism of pain, we could still ask why it is lived in just this way. The gap may say something about the nature of reality, about our concepts, or only about knowledge that is still incomplete. By itself it does not decide among those possibilities.</p>
<p>Frank Jackson, in <em>Epiphenomenal Qualia</em> (<em>The Philosophical Quarterly</em>, 1982), introduced Mary, a scientist who knows every physical fact about colour vision and lives in a black-and-white environment. Does she learn something new when she sees colour for the first time? One answer says she gains a new fact. Another says she gains a new ability, or direct acquaintance with what she already knew by description. The experiment opens a problem. It is not a generally accepted refutation of physicalism.</p>
<p>Anaesthesia, brain injury and changes in brain activity are strong evidence of a close relation between brain and consciousness. Correlation, causal dependence and identity are nevertheless three different claims. Unexplained does not mean supernatural. A description of function need not yet be an explanation of experience. Both shortcuts end the discussion too soon.</p>
<h2>Four options, no winning slogan</h2>
<p>Physicalism says that everything is physical or wholly depends on the physical. Its difficulty is to explain experience without leaving it out. Dualism says that the mental and the physical cannot be identified without remainder, and then has to explain their relation, especially causal interaction. Idealism gives ontological priority to the mental or the experiential and has to explain a stable shared world that does not depend on one individual. Panpsychism attributes an experiential aspect to the basic constituents of reality and meets the combination problem: a sum of many perspectives is not automatically one perspective.</p>
<p>These names are not uniform. Physicalism need not deny experience. Property dualism is not the same as two independent substances. Idealism need not claim that my personal mind manufactures the world. Neutral monism takes the mental and the physical as two appearances of a basis that itself need be neither. Emergence, the arising of a property at a higher level of organisation, may be weak and compatible with physicalism, or strong and irreducible. The word “arises” explains nothing by itself.</p>
<p>A good theory has to clarify more than its own slogan. I ask what it explains, what it only renames, and what new problem it pays for that with.</p>
<h2>A change in experience is not a change in the cosmos</h2>
<p>Everything we know reaches us through experience. It does not follow that everything exists only as experience. What is first in knowing need not be first in existing. I know my own pain differently from the activity of neurons. A difference of access is not yet a difference of basic nature.</p>
<p>When attention, appraisal or mood changes, the lived world changes. Action may change, and through it the surroundings. After meditation a conflict may feel less threatening. If I then answer more calmly, the conflict itself may change. That sequence is real. It is not proof that consciousness makes all matter. Nor is it “merely subjective” in a sense that would cancel the change.</p>
<p>The claim that an intention alters a distant event with no known mediation is already an empirical hypothesis. The outcome, the comparison and the way of excluding chance, selective reporting and unconscious influence all have to be specified. A vague gesture at quantum physics is neither a mechanism nor evidence.</p>
<p>A real change in experience, a real change in behaviour and a claim about the basic nature of the cosmos are three different things.</p>
<h2>An experience of unity and its interpretation</h2>
<p>A sense of unity, of lost boundaries of the self, or of extraordinary clarity can be profound. Intensity does not by itself decide that the interpretation is right. The report “I lived through a state in which the difference between me and the world seemed weakened” is not the same statement as “the universe is ontologically a single consciousness”. William James, in <em>The Varieties of Religious Experience</em> (1902), described the noetic quality of mystical states as one of their typical marks. The feeling of having known the truth directly is still part of the experience. To convince someone else, it needs a further argument.</p>
<p>Language, tradition and expectation shape the interpretation. One person speaks of God, another of an impersonal Absolute, a third of a change in brain activity. Shared features of such experiences are interesting. The differences among them cannot be declared irrelevant in advance. A description of a brain mechanism does not exhaust the meaning of the experience. The personal meaning of the experience is not, in turn, an indubitable cosmology. An experience can change one’s relation to anxiety, death or other people and still fail to provide an infallible map of the world. A beneficial effect does not by itself show that every belief attached to the experience is true.</p>
<p>The working protocol is short. Describe the experience. Name the interpretation. State the alternatives. Write the reasons for and against. Decide what would change the belief. Only then consider a wider conclusion. If a system explains every objection by the critic’s insufficient spiritual development, it has closed itself to correction. A medical title does not by itself prove a cosmology. A tradition can matter culturally and still be wrong in a given claim.</p>
<p>A similar vocabulary is not proof that one teaching is at work. Spirit, consciousness, energy and development do not mean the same thing in every tradition. A wide map, such as Aurobindo’s integral yoga or Wilber’s integral theory, can be useful and still fail to confirm every ontological piece of itself. A passing state of consciousness is not the same as lasting maturity. Maturity shows more in how a person bears disagreement, corrects an error and handles power. A numerical scale of consciousness derived from a muscle test is not an established scientific measure of spiritual level or of the truth of claims.</p>
<h2>Who remains when I change</h2>
<p>Personal identity asks what makes a human being the same person over time. It is not the question of whether he feels unchanged. Two people can share the same properties and still not be one person. A person can change sharply and still be counted as identical with his younger self. Numerical identity and qualitative similarity are different relations.</p>
<p>Bodily theories hold to the continuity of the organism or the brain. Psychological theories hold to the continuity of memory, intention and character, and have to take care not to presuppose the very identity they mean to explain. A substantial soul offers a bearer that lasts behind the changes. Naming it is not yet explaining how we would recognise it, or why it is the same soul. Memory is not an archive: memories are reconstructed. Losing some memories does not yet mean a new person. A convincing memory is not yet a guarantee that the event happened as it is recalled.</p>
<p>Derek Parfit, in <em>Reasons and Persons</em> (1984), examined whether psychological relations can be what matters for a practical concern with the future even where strict identity does not yield a single answer. It does not follow that the person does not matter. It follows that we have to ask whether the whole value of survival must be carried by one indivisible fact of identity. Two equally faithful copies can be psychological continuations of one life and still cannot both be the same original person. The same distinction is needed for reincarnation: similarity of memories or character does not by itself prove identity across two lives.</p>
<p>A life story gives experience a meaning and need not be accurate. Changing it can be healing. Narrative identity is self-understanding, not automatically ontological identity. Loosening attachment to one’s self-image can change a person’s relation to the world. It does not mean he ceases to be responsible. The sentence “the ego is an illusion” can mean that there is no unchanging core. It need not mean that there is no experience, no relations and no consequences of acts.</p>
<h2>The present is not proof that only now exists</h2>
<p>The advice to attend to the present moment is practical. The claim that only the present exists is ontological. The one does not prove the other. The presentist theory says that only present events exist, and then has to explain why it is true that something happened when that event no longer exists, and what a shared now would be. Eternalism takes past, present and future as parts of reality and has to explain the experience of passage. The growing-block theory leaves the future not yet in existence and has to say what fixes the present edge.</p>
<p>The special theory of relativity does not provide one cosmic simultaneity independent of a frame of reference. That complicates the simple picture of a single now for the whole universe. Physics alone, without a further philosophical argument, does not close the ontology of time. Eternalism is not fatalism either. The question of which events exist is not the question of whether earlier states uniquely determine them. Decisions in a life can have effects even if the future, in some sense, already belongs in the picture of reality.</p>
<p>Eternity can mean endless duration or timelessness. These are not synonyms. A weakened sense of duration in deep concentration is a report about the experience of time, not proof that a person has stepped outside physical time. Living in the present should not mean denying memory, plans and commitments. Three questions have to be kept apart: what exists in time, how we experience time, and what determines events.</p>
<h2>Freedom is not a quarrel about one sentence</h2>
<p>Determinism says that the state of the world and the laws fix its further course. Predictability says what we can calculate. Fatalism says that the outcome will occur regardless of what we do. They are not synonyms. Even in a deterministic model our decisions can be among the causes of the outcome.</p>
<p>Compatibilism takes freedom to be compatible with determinism: acting on one’s own reasons, without relevant coercion, with a capacity for self-control. The objection is that such a person is still not the ultimate origin of his own character. Metaphysical libertarianism requires a freedom incompatible with determinism and has to explain why indeterminacy is not mere chance. Scepticism about free will questions the freedom required for basic moral desert. It need not thereby deny upbringing, protection or a change in behaviour.</p>
<p>In 1983 Benjamin Libet and colleagues published in <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6640273"><em>Brain</em></a> a paper on the time of conscious intention and the readiness potential. Participants made a simple spontaneous movement and reported the moment at which they became aware of the intention. The measured preparatory activity preceded the reported moment. It does not follow that every long-term or morally weighty decision is finished before awareness. The timing estimate is imprecise, and it is not clear what the preparatory signal exactly is. Some models read it as fluctuating activity accumulating to a movement threshold. An experiment about a finger movement is not a verdict on the whole metaphysics of freedom.</p>
<p>Practical freedom is a capacity that can be developed: knowing the options, regulating emotion, interrupting an automatic reaction, acting on considered reasons. Coercion, addiction, impaired cognition and circumstance can limit it. Strengthening it still matters while the metaphysical question stays open. Understanding the causes of behaviour does not mean approving the harm. It can shift the response from bare retaliation toward protection, repair and prevention. The voice of the person harmed, and the demand for justice, remain. Freedom does not show itself only at the moment of choice. It also shows itself in the habits, knowledge and conditions of decision that we build over time.</p>
<h2>What is left on Monday</h2>
<p>Ethics does not simply drop out of one sentence about the nature of the world. An idealist may see in another person an expression of a shared ground, a physicalist the vulnerability of an experiencing organism, a dualist the dignity of a person. A similar compassion can grow from different metaphysics. None of them by itself guarantees good conduct.</p>
<p>Spiritual bypassing uses a spiritual interpretation to avoid pain, conflict or responsibility. The sentence that everything is a lesson, or that a person attracts his own suffering, can silence needed help and shift the blame onto the one who suffers. Meaning can be sought without denying causes and needs. If a metaphysical reading dulls sensitivity to concrete suffering, or justifies power without check, we have to examine not only its logic but its use.</p>
<p>Idealism or panpsychism alone does not entail that my memories and personal identity survive death. A spiritual experience does not guarantee a particular fate after death either. Distinguishing hope from knowledge does not cheapen hope. Uncertainty can lead to humility and to attention toward what is available: relationships, care, making and truthfulness. I do not have to solve the whole of ontology in order to know that pain weighs on the one who suffers, and that my action can help or harm.</p>
<p>The five themes bear on one another and still do not solve one another. Even if consciousness were a basic feature of reality, the question would remain why this subject exists and what grounds its continuity. Universal consciousness and personal survival are not the same thesis. The picture of an already existing future does not yet mean that deciding is ineffective.</p>
<p>The working stance I take from the volume is not a finished ontology. Respect the world, respect experience, and allow for error. That is not relativism. A better argument and more reliable evidence carry more weight. Humility is a confidence proportionate to the reasons, and a willingness to correct oneself. Experience is the starting point of questions, not an automatic answer to them. The depth of metaphysics also shows in how precisely it can name its own limits.</p>
<h2>Six steps for one Sunday reading</h2>
<p>The frame is not a test of spiritual rank. It does not require an extraordinary state.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Choose a concrete experience.</strong> The taste of tea, a memory, a decision, a feeling of calm. Describe what appeared. Separate observation from a guess about the cause.</li>
<li><strong>Name the assumption.</strong> Finish the sentence: I think this phenomenon is… Then decide whether that is a description, a causal explanation or an ontological claim.</li>
<li><strong>Build the strongest alternative.</strong> Not a caricature. How a well-read physicalist, idealist or sceptic would explain the phenomenon, and what their explanation would handle better.</li>
<li><strong>Name the kind of evidence.</strong> Personal experience, measurement, a logical argument, a historical record. What would weaken the claim. Even an untestable thesis can be weighed by coherence and explanatory power.</li>
<li><strong>Look at the practical reach.</strong> Would accepting the view change action? Does it bring responsibility and openness, or mainly a feeling of being exceptional? Put the same question to the opposing view.</li>
<li><strong>Write a proportionate conclusion.</strong> Distinguish: I know, I have good reason to think, I incline to, I do not yet know. Leave one question open.</li>
</ol>
<p>In conversation, first restate the other person’s view so that he recognises it as accurate. Then separate agreement about the description from disagreement about the explanation. One well-formed objection is usually more useful than ten quick counter-arguments. And the question the volume leaves for today: what in ordinary life would actually change if my favourite metaphysical theory were wrong?</p>
<p>A related reflection on a conviction firm enough to act on and still open to revision is in <a href="article.php?slug=dobry-skeptik-viac-nez-pochybnost">A good sceptic has more than doubt and less than certainty</a>.</p>
<p><em>This is a philosophical reading of the second volume of Sunday metaphysics, dated 4 October 2026. It is not medical advice and not a clinical account of consciousness, anaesthesia or free will. The neuroscientific and historical references are a guide to the literature the volume uses: Chalmers 1995, Levine 1983, Jackson 1982, James 1902, Parfit 1984, and Libet and colleagues 1983. They do not replace a judgement about a particular person.</em></p>
